ur  creaky old system to stick you with even  more delays in the future we could avoid  that fate with better technology that  would allow us to fly more planes and do  it safely so why can't the government  bring the system into the 21st century  welcome to the John Murtha Johnstown  Cambria County Airport with a seven  million dollar air traffic control tower  a 14 million dollar hangar and an 18  million dollar runway the problem is how  politicians spend money this is the John  Murtha Airport at 10:30 in the morning  there is nobody here seems like the  Pennsylvania Airport attracts more  reporters than passengers even so the  airport was just awarded another eight  hundred thousand dollars in federal  stimulus money to repave an alternate  runway so much scrutiny over is pork  Stravaganza but Congressman John Murtha  is proud of his Airport  Martha's earmarking of grants for his  Airport in Johnstown is is a you know a  classic example of the excesses that  Congress does maybe the only way to get  the politics out of our air traffic  system is to take the system away from  the politicians some say we should let a  corporation manage the skies people are  talking about running our air traffic  control system like a big private  corporation that means putting profits  before people the air traffic  controllers Union runs ads criticizing  the proposal  and to many it sounds like a far-out  free-market idea to have a private  company running air navigation system  but it turns out Canada disagrees Nav  Canada is the private company that runs  Canada's air navigation system the  second biggest in the world here in  Canada  folks often take pride in their nation's  lavish government programs yet Canadians  allow Nav Canada a private company to  manage their skies and the idea of  running the air traffic control system  like a business often called  commercialization enjoys the support of  some prominent Democrats one of the  biggest champions of this during the  Clin